<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kavirajamarga</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">9th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kavirajamarga ('<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-road">Royal Road</a> of Poets') is the earliest surviving literary work in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a>, a treatise on poetics written in the 9th century at the court of the Rashtrakuta emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amoghavarsha-i">Amoghavarsha I</a>. Attributed to the king himself or to his court poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srivijaya">Srivijaya</a>, it adapts <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> theories of poetry to Kannada, praises the land between the Kaveri and Godavari rivers, and mentions earlier Kannada writers whose works are lost.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kavirajamarga.jpg" alt="Kavirajamarga" width="320" height="125" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Auguel · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stanza_from_Kavirajamarga.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
